add('E.11','Replacement data imply all intermediate comparisons','Absent: no R_S with a natural weak-equivalence unit is constructed, and the fibre axioms (a),(b),(c) are not stated. The ordinary full-subcategory embeddings on 115 scans012–014 do not provide such replacement data.','B062|217|218|remplacement','DER_XIIc-a.tex, ms.217–218, companion B062')
add('E.12','Six-object failure of the intermediate-subcategory implication','Absent: these cotes contain neither the finite lattice nor the (a)+(b)⇒(c) assertion. The unrelated gluing caution on 115 scan014 is not this finite localization counterexample.','B062|217|218','DER_XIIc-a.tex, ms.217–218, companion B062')
add('E.13','Homotopy categories do not recover coherent gluing','115 scan014 warns that two pieces “ne sont pas néct. ss. pl. … Σ′” and that N′→N need not be an equivalence. This is an ordinary categorical gluing warning, not the row’s loss of faithfulness from Ho Fun([1],V) to Fun([1],Ho V); that enhanced counterexample is absent.','recollement|cohérent|C.7','Dossier PATHS D.20; STRATA C.7; comparison passage DER_XIIb-a.tex, ms.59–63','MPL_150_085-099+151_001-031 §106 (coherent-gluing lead only)')
add('E.14','Nullification, modalities and local cartesian closure','Absent: no accessible reflection of spaces, dependent-sum closure or LCC criterion is stated. Cartesian closedness of an ordinary presheaf topos behind 115 scan004’s internal Homs is a different assertion from local cartesian closure of the localized coefficient category.','modalité|cartésien|localisation','Dossier POLES E.2–E.3; no manuscript page independently specified')
add('F.1','Der1–4 and three homotopy Kan functors','115 scan010 displays the ordinary pairing adjunctions involving f_!,f* and fᵒ_!, while 113 scan004 discusses an adjoint triple in additive presheaf categories. These are direct formal precursors; the derivator axioms, coherent comma formulas and pasting theorem are absent.','adjoint|changement de base|138','DER_I-a.tex; DER_VI-b.tex, ms.138–145; DER_VI-c.tex, ms.146–169','BISITE F.18 (higher extension remains open)')
add('F.2','Strict smooth/proper base change and converses','108 scan004 item12 writes “f fib, g∈W⇒g′∈W” for a cartesian square, and 112 scan004 has smooth/proper labels. Neither is a comparison between Kan composites, and the two base-change isomorphisms and their universal converses are absent.','changement de base|lisse|propre','DER_VI-b.tex, ms.138–145; DER_VI-c.tex, ms.146–169; dossier STRATA D.4')
add('F.3','All strict pullback squares are not homotopy exact','Absent: no endpoint-inclusion coefficient counterexample is supplied. The ordinary identities on 115 scan010 involve a single functor and its adjunctions; they do not assert unrestricted base change for a strict square.','cartésien|changement de base','Dossier Lead3; thematic DER_VI-b.tex, ms.138–145; DER_VI-c.tex, ms.146–169')
add('F.4','Smooth/internal and general/external projection formulas','115 scan004 has an external product L⊠M and 115 scan010 has ordinary coend/pairing adjunctions. The derived monoidal projection theorem, including its smooth-map or locally constant-coefficient hypotheses, is absent; none follows from the literal full-faithfulness assertion about ⊠, which itself fails in the unrestricted Set case.','projection|tensoriel|produit','Dossier Lead3 and VI projection formulas; no more precise ms locator supplied')
add('F.5','Monoidality does not give every internal projection formula','Absent: the dossier’s homotopy-theoretic counterexample is not written. The overstrong exponent assertion on 115 scan004 is a separate ordinary-category warning; it cannot be identified with this projection-formula failure.','projection|tensoriel','Dossier Lead3; thematic VI projection formulas')
add('F.6','Right projection needs dualizability or finite limits','Absent: there is no dualizable coefficient hypothesis, homotopy-finite fibre argument or right derived projection comparison. The inverse-limit-preserving functor categories in 115 scans004–006 are ordinary categories and do not supply the row’s stable exactness input.','projection|limites|dual','Dossier Lead3; thematic DER_VI-c.tex, Kan-exchange discussion')
add('F.7','Internal-Hom exchange and mates','115 scan004 explicitly defines Hom_A(X,Y)=p_{B*}Hom_{A×B}(X,Y), and 115 scan010 gives the two pairing adjunctions. These are the closest ordinary precursors to mate/exchange calculations, but smooth f^!, derived coherence and the base-change mate of a strict square are absent.','Hom|adjoint|échange','Dossier VI exchange formulas; no precise ms locator independently supplied')
add('F.8','Both base and coefficient opposition reverse adjunctions','115 scan015 explicitly writes F(X,M)ᵒ≃F^∨(X,Mᵒ) and says the two constructions are “duals|duales l’une de l’autre”. This directly anticipates the formal two-opposition mechanism; it is not a self-duality of fixed coefficients over a fixed base, and the derived smooth/proper consequences are absent.','opposé|dualité|84','DER_VI-b.tex, ms.84')
add('F.9','Pointed open–closed extensions and stable recollement','Absent: 115 scan014 glues ordinary full subcategories but contains no complementary sieve/cosieve, extension by zero, or coherent fibre–cofibre sequence. The exact open–closed theorem is stronger than that gluing sketch.','ouvert|fermé|recollement','DER_I-a.tex, ms.1–9; dossier VI open–closed formulas','STRATA 0.1 (related variance); exact F.9 absent')
add('F.10','Enhanced correspondence six-operation formalism','115 scans004–006 provide Set-valued kernels and equivalent functor-category presentations, while scan010 supplies ordinary pairings. This is a credible correspondence precursor, but spans with a smooth leg, composition through homotopy-exact squares, and a lax symmetric monoidal infinity-functor are absent.','correspondance|noyau|foncteur','Dossier Lead3 and Mann question; compare DER_XIIb-a.tex, ms.59–63 for ordinary crossing kernels','MPL_105 — BISITE F.10 (expressly an unpursued lead)')
add('F.11','Cohomological étaleness and finite-bifibration ambidexterity','Absent: no identification f^!=f* or finite-biproduct comparison f_!≃f_* is stated. The opposite functor-category equivalences in 115 scan015 reverse variance and coefficients; they do not identify the two Kan adjoints of a fixed functor.','étale|bifibration|dualité','Dossier Lead3 terminology; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('F.12','Smooth maps need not be diagonal-closed','Absent: these cotes do not specify a geometric-setup convention or test diagonal closure. The diagonal-looking Hom kernels of 115 are objects on products, not diagonals of smooth base functors.','diagonale|lisse','Dossier Mann versus later conventions; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('F.13','Multiderivator correspondences need an enhancement','115 scan006’s finite partitions and scan009’s crossed-out two-cell diagrams suggest multivariable and higher-functorial ambitions. No fibered multiderivator, 2-multicategory of correspondences, or higher enhancement is constructed, so the cited comparison theorem is absent.','multidérivateur|correspondance|mult','Dossier Hörmann comparison; no separate ms locator supplied','BISITE F.18; GALOIS E.11/G.6 (related open constructions)')
add('F.14','Relative smooth/proper calibrations depend on the base','Absent: no indexed infinity-category or relative calibration definition occurs. In particular the ordinary product category A×B in 115 scan004 says nothing about replacing strict categorical pullbacks by pullbacks in Cat∞.','lisse|propre|relative','Dossier Anel–Weinberger comparison; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('F.15','Genus-zero exit category and geometric coefficients','Absent: none of the five cotes mentions punctured spheres, mapping-class groups, multicurves, or the genus-zero moduli-space stratification. The general kernel language of 115 does not supply this particular geometric dictionary.','genre|multicourbe|strate','Dossier STRATA genus-zero comparison; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('F.16','Forgetting a mark is proper but not always smooth','Absent: the forgetting-mark functor and its Bℤ lifting category are not on these pages. The c-propre/c-lisse labels in 112 scan004 are unrelated to that specific moduli-space example.','marque|genre|propre','Dossier STRATA forgetting-mark question; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('F.17','Geometric direct image and boundary base change','Absent: no boundary cosieve in a genus-zero entrance category or constructible-sheaf direct-image comparison appears. The single-functor ordinary adjunctions in 115 scan010 do not establish topological proper base change.','bord|directe|genre','Dossier STRATA geometric direct image and boundary base change; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('F.18','Strict higher-index derivator base change remains open','115 scan009 sketches opposite two-cells between topoi, but does not state a higher Der4 or invariance of lax comma constructions. The open target remains absent as a solution; even the wheel’s equivalences in scan006 concern ordinary Set-valued functor categories.','Der|supérieure|comma','Dossier strict nCat-indexing question; no separate ms locator supplied','BISITE F.18; BISITE C.10')
add('F.19','Finite discrete bifibrations are the exact proper class','Absent: no cohomological properness definition, finite-fibre criterion, or canonical biproduct map is given. Cote115’s ordinary presheaf duality does not classify maps on which left and right derived Kan extensions agree.','bifibration|fini|propre','Dossier Mann proper-class question; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('G.1','Stability gives spectral weighted limits','Absent: no spectrum action, stable derivator or commutation of homotopy-finite limits with arbitrary homotopy colimits is constructed. 115 scan015’s additive notation and 112’s complexes do not by themselves provide that coherent spectral structure.','stable|spectre|limites','Dossier Lead4 stable-derivator input; no separate ms locator supplied','GALOIS E.11; G.6 (related general-coefficient boundary)')
add('G.2','Covariant spectral dualizing weight','115 scan008 gives the ordinary contraction Φ*_AΨ and evaluation identities, a genuine weighted-colimit precursor. The covariant spectral weight w_S and derived coend formula for a homotopy limit are absent; H_A and H^A in that scan must not be renamed a derived dualizing object.','dualisant|coend|poids','Dossier Lead4 dualizing-weight clause; compare DER_XIIb-a.tex, ms.68 for ordinary coend transport')
add('G.3','A pointwise dualizing twist is insufficient','Absent: no pointwise spectral twist formula or one-arrow counterexample is stated. The ordinary Hom_A/H^A kernel distinction in 115 scan008 supports caution about variance, but does not itself refute the derived claim.','dualisant|tensoriel|ponctuel','Dossier Lead4 exaggerated objectwise interpretation; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('G.4','Derived kernels compose by coends and satisfy exact base change','115 scans004–008 supply ordinary kernels X∈Â×B-hat, currying and contraction, and scan010 supplies their Kan-pairing compatibility. This is a substantial formal precursor; spectral weights, derived coend composition and homotopy-exact-square base change are absent.','noyau|coend|transitivité','Dossier PartG compatibility; compare DER_XIIb-a.tex, ms.59–63,68 for ordinary collage/coend machinery','MPL_105 — BISITE F.10 (related correspondence lead)')
add('G.5','No unrestricted restriction of dualizing weights','Absent: no restriction formula i*w_S≃w_R or finite-poset counterexample appears. The ambiguous arrows labelled as functoriality of the envelope in 115 scan012 concern an ordinary completion and cannot supply an extraordinary pullback for spectral weights.','dualisant|restriction|base','Dossier PartG unrestricted weight base change; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('G.6','Perfect coefficient duality reverses variance','115 scan015 explicitly opposes the coefficient category, but does not assert Spanier–Whitehead or derived A-linear duality. Finite spectra, perfect objects, the opposite coefficient ring, and preservation of perfectness by finite homotopy limits/colimits are absent; an ordinary formal opposition is weaker than this coefficient duality.','parfait|dualité|coefficients','Dossier PartG finite subcategories; no separate ms locator supplied','GALOIS E.11; G.6 (distinct IDs, related higher-coefficient question)')
add('G.7','Finite EI categories are not uniformly homotopy finite','Absent: the BC₂/F₂ example and failure to preserve countable coproducts are not supplied. The finite-index-family assumption on 115 scan006 is a combinatorial precaution, not a statement that all finite EI categories admit spectral weighted limits.','EI|coproduit|fini','Dossier PartG finite-EI scope; no separate ms locator supplied','STRATA B.2 (related EI dictionary, not this weighted-limit claim)')
add('G.8','Finite-group ambidexterity needs invertible group order','Absent: there is no norm map, group-order invertibility assumption or unbounded-complex argument. The abelian or k-linear presheaf sketches in 112,113,115 do not imply ambidexterity without this coefficient-sensitive input.','norme|groupe|inversible','Dossier PartG coefficient-sensitive finite-EI class; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('G.9','Canonical finite-poset duality needs an upper-interval criterion','Absent: no finite-poset transform V_P, Gorenstein* criterion or strict upper-interval calculation occurs. The ordinary self-dual envelope suggested in 115 scans011–014 is a different construction and gives no equivalence criterion for this stable transform.','Gorenstein|intervalle|poset','Dossier Lead4 corrected equivalence and PartG finite posets; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('G.10','Least-vertex constructible-sheaf dictionary','Absent: these pages contain no barycentric least-vertex map, conical stratification or exit-category computation. Thus their presheaf formulas cannot by themselves identify ordinary diagram categories with the specified geometric constructible sheaves.','sommet|strate|constructible','Dossier STRATA A.4,A.5; PartG prerequisite dictionary','BISITE G.10; STRATA A.4; STRATA 0.1')
add('G.11','Minimum constructibility is not Verdier-stable','Absent: no Verdier dual, least-vertex strata or [1] geometric counterexample appears. Cote115’s formal opposition and Isbell-like conjugacy must not be interpreted as ordinary Verdier duality preserving one fixed constructible subcategory.','Verdier|constructible|minimum','Dossier Lead4 unconditional geometric identification; no separate ms locator supplied','BISITE G.10; D.13 (related positive dictionaries, not this counterexample)')
add('G.12','Correct geometric Verdier comparison includes cosheaf pushforward','Absent: although 115 scan015 distinguishes “cofaisceaux” from “faisceaux”, it gives no geometric cosheaf pushforward, upper-interval hypothesis or cellular identification. The row’s explicit s₊V_Zs* formula is a substantially stronger stable/geometric assertion.','Verdier|cofaisceau|recollement','Dossier PartG positive geometric identification; no separate ms locator supplied','BISITE G.10; D.13 (related geometric claims)')
add('G.13','Weighted-limit duality does not extend unstably','The constructions in 115 scans004–010 are Set-valued and ordinary, with no claim that arbitrary finite limits of spaces are a fixed weighted colimit. The row’s unstable impossibility statement is absent; conversely its stable conclusions cannot be retroactively attributed to this ordinary kernel wheel.','stable|limite|poids','Dossier PartG unstable clause; no separate ms locator supplied')
add('G.14','Tree-orientation equivalences use tilting data','Absent: no finite tree, reflection functor or pseudonatural stable equivalence is constructed. The canonical opposition of ordinary envelope categories in 115 scan012 is not a proof that all orientations of a tree are strongly stably equivalent.','arbre|réflexion|tilting','Dossier Lead4 Groth–Šťovíček contribution; no separate ms locator supplied')
# Final cross-cote check: preserve the positive additive and derived-contraction precursors in113.
notes['BISITE E.3']['note']='113 scan004 says “assez de projectifs” and displays ordinary additive free cocompletion, while113 scan013(a) gives a sum of represented projectives and115 scan015 writes Hom(A,M)≃Hom_!(Â,M). These are direct ordinary-category precursors, but the row’s coherent small-colimit-preserving mapping-space generators and enhanced slice presentations are absent.'
notes['BISITE F.4']['note']='115 scan004 has L⊠M, and113 scan012 boxes (L⊗_k x)*_kL′≃(L*_kL′)⊗_k x, explicitly “à vérifier” by represented inputs. These are ordinary Set/additive projection-type precursors; the derived monoidal theorem, including its smooth-map or locally constant-coefficient hypotheses, is absent, and it does not follow from the false unrestricted full-faithfulness assertion about ⊠ on115 scan004.'
notes['BISITE F.7']['note']='115 scan004 defines Hom_A(X,Y)=p_{B*}Hom_{A×B}(X,Y),115 scan010 gives pairing adjunctions, and113 scan012 boxes two Hom/tensor adjunctions. These are close ordinary Set/additive precursors to mate/exchange calculations, but smooth f^!, coherent derived base change and its mate for a strict square are absent.'
notes['BISITE F.8']['note']='115 scan015 writes F(X,M)ᵒ≃F∨(X,Mᵒ) and “duals|duales l’une de l’autre”, while113 scan006 exchanges additive contraction and internal Hom after opposing M. This directly anticipates the formal two-opposition mechanism; a same-base self-duality of fixed coefficients and the derived smooth/proper consequences are absent.'
notes['BISITE F.13']['note']='113 scan003’s “MU-fibré M1”,115 scan006’s finite variance partitions and115 scan009’s cancelled two-cell diagrams suggest multivariable and higher-functorial ambitions. No fibered multiderivator, 2-multicategory of correspondences or higher enhancement is constructed, so the cited comparison theorem is absent.'
notes['BISITE G.2']['note']='115 scan008 gives the ordinary contraction Φ*_AΨ, and113 scan013 proposes a derived additive contraction using projective resolutions with an explicit flatness condition. These are genuine weighted-pairing precursors, but the covariant spectral weight w_S and its derived coend formula for a homotopy limit are absent; H_A/H^A cannot be renamed that dualizing weight.'
notes['BISITE G.4']['note']='115 scans004–010 provide ordinary kernels, currying, contraction and Kan-pairing compatibility, while113 scan013 treats a derived additive contraction under projective/flatness hypotheses. The spectral kernels K_u, their derived coend composition/transitivity identities and homotopy-exact-square base change are absent; those extra assertions do not follow from either precursor.'
notes['BISITE G.6']['note']='115 scan015 opposes the coefficient category, and113 scan006 exchanges additive tensor and Hom operations using Mᵒ; neither asserts Spanier–Whitehead or derived A-linear duality. Finite spectra, perfect-object hypotheses, the opposite coefficient ring and finite-homotopy preservation of perfectness are absent, so the formal oppositions are weaker than this coefficient duality.'
assert set(notes)=={r['id'] for r in rows}, (set(notes)^ {r['id'] for r in rows})
state=Path('context/notes/RESULTS_STATE.md').read_text()
# Search exact IDs independently, and a thematic expression within the supplied companion.
# Search hits are a reproducible lead log, not evidence that each historical claim is reviewed.
audit=[]
for r in rows:
 n=notes[r['id']]
 exact=subprocess.run(['rg','-n','-F',r['id'],'context/notes/RESULTS_STATE.md','context/DOSSIER_STATEMENTS_WITH_STATUS.md'],capture_output=True,text=True)
 thematic=subprocess.run(['rg','-n','-i','-m','2',n['query'],'context/companion/chunks'],capture_output=True,text=True)
 hits=thematic.stdout.splitlines()
 audit.append({'id':r['id'],'query':n['query'],'exact_matches':exact.stdout.splitlines(),'companion_hits':hits[:24],'companion_hits_truncated':len(hits)>24,'reference_scope':'Rows retain the dossier claims; searches supply contextual leads, not independent proof verification.'})
 if not n['state']:
  exactstate=bool(re.search(r'\*\*'+re.escape(r['id'])+r'\*\*',state))
  n['state']=r['id'] if exactstate else 'No exact entry in RESULTS_STATE; preserve dossier row status.'
 r.update(n)
(base/'research_bisite_115.coverage.json').write_text(json.dumps(rows,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
(base/'research_bisite_115.search_audit.json').write_text(json.dumps(audit,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
(base/'research_bisite_115.coverage.txt').write_text('\n'.join(r['id'] for r in rows)+'\n')
header='''# Research comparison: every supplied BISITE row and cote 115

Research-only draft for integration; the frozen transcription was not changed and no scan image was read by this researcher. The comparison uses `deliverable/MPL_108+112+113+114+115_transcription.tex` and its frozen page fragments, after the context-open gate. The full context README, full `context/notes/RESULTS_STATE.md`, and `context/hua_project/README.md` were read before drafting; Hua interpretations are treated as credited leads, never as replacements for frozen readings.

## Status and coverage convention

Every row below cites **`context/DOSSIER_STATEMENTS_WITH_STATUS.md`, the exact ID shown**; its “claim (return: …)” status and original locator are copied verbatim into the table. The dossier expressly says **none of these returns has had a full-effort review**. “Proved”, “Known”, and “Refuted” therefore remain the return’s claims, not fresh certifications here; “absent” concerns this five-cote frozen selection and does not claim absence from the larger archive.

The comparison is exhaustive for the supplied BISITE section: **113 IDs: A.1–A.34, B.1–B.22, C.1–C.10, E.1–E.14, F.1–F.19, G.1–G.14**. No BISITE D rows are present in that dossier, although RESULTS_STATE separately mentions D.13. Every context column names the supplied companion file and manuscript locator when available; a locator such as “Lead3” is retained as such rather than inventing a manuscript page. `DER_*.tex` paths below are relative to `context/companion/chunks/`; state references are IDs in `context/notes/RESULTS_STATE.md`, whose selective contents do not supersede omitted dossier rows.

The companion references locate historical discussion or the dossier’s stated source; they do **not** claim that a return’s modern proof appears verbatim at that locus. Exact-ID and thematic context searches for each row are recorded in the companion JSON audit; the exact source strings and IDs are also preserved in JSON and a plain-text manifest.

'''
def esc(x):return x.replace('|','\\|').replace('\n',' ')
parts=[header]
for g in 'ABCEFG':
 parts.append('## BISITE '+g+'\n\n| Exact ID / claim | Return status (unreviewed) | Original locator | Comparison to frozen selection (1–4 sentences) | Supplied context and current state |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n')
 for r in rows:
  if r['id'].startswith('BISITE '+g+'.'):
   parts.append('| '+esc(r['id']+' — '+r['title'])+' | '+esc(r['status'])+' | '+esc(r['locator'])+' | '+esc(r['note'])+' | '+esc(r['context']+'. RESULTS_STATE: '+r['state'])+' |\n')
 parts.append('\n')
(base/'research_bisite_115.md').write_text(''.join(parts))
print('Wrote exact-ID table and audit for',len(rows),'rows.')
